Hand deep work up after 1024 nesting levels instead of 8192 (fuzzing)

Pestka's checker recurses one native frame chain per level of nested work
(whnf of a major, an argument's type, a Nat argument, a closure) and hands
what lies past `deepAt` levels up to the outermost level, where it is
worked out on a shallow stack (OneDeepJob). Natively main runs on a
256 MiB thread; under wasmfuzz the generated code's frames live on the
engine's 32 MiB native stack, and the arena fixture
perf__unfold-before-literal-bad traps with a stack overflow (at 8192)
before it reaches the bound. At 1024 levels it does not. The bound only
moves where work resumes, not what is checked: the 226 arena fixtures
give the same verdicts at 1024 as at 8192 (130 accepted, 96 rejected;
native build of the harness). The `verify deepAt` example follows the
constant. Applied to the Aver source before the crate is generated (Aver
has no cfg), so this build only.
Upstream: none (a fuzzing limit; no issues upstream, checked 2026-11-22).

diff --git a/check/tc.av b/check/tc.av
index 11c3ea3..ddb8816 100644
--- a/check/tc.av
+++ b/check/tc.av
@@ -1881,10 +1881,10 @@ decision OneDeepJob
 
 fn deepAt() -> Int
     ? "How deep work may nest before what it needs is handed up to the outermost level (OneDeepJob)."
-    8192
+    1024
 
 verify deepAt
-    deepAt() => 8192
+    deepAt() => 1024
 
 fn jobDone(j: Job, st: St) -> Result<St, Fail>
     ? "At the outermost level (OneDeepJob), a job handed up from too deep worked out on a shallow stack, from the state it came with set back as finishing would leave it, and its result held for the rest of the declaration (HandedUpHeld): a whnf, a type, or the answer for a pair. Each outermost level takes the state out of the failure in its own match, so that it has one owner (HandedUpHeld)."
